     m4 - macro processor

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     mm44 [_f_i_l_e ...]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     mm44 copies each named _f_i_l_e in turn to the standard output,
     expanding macros as it goes; a _f_i_l_e named - means the stan-
     dard input, and if no _f_i_l_e is given at all, mm44 reads the
     standard input.

     mm44 copies its input to the output until it finds a potential
     macro: a string of letters, digits, and underscores that
     begins with a non-digit and is surrounded by non-al-
     phanumerics.  If the string names a defined macro, mm44
     removes it (and, if the next character is (, a paren-
     thesized, comma-separated argument list, collected by
     processing it exactly like other text so that an argument
     may itself contain macro calls) from the input, and replaces
     it with the macro's value.  Up to nine arguments are kept
     per call; commas inside quotes or balanced parentheses do
     not separate arguments, and leading unquoted whitespace in
     an argument is discarded.  If the string does not name a
     defined macro, it is copied through unchanged.

     A pair of quote characters (by default the backquote ` and
     the apostrophe ') inhibits macro recognition of the text be-
     tween them; one level of quotes is stripped away wherever mm44
     encounters it, and quotes nest.

     A user-defined macro's value is the text of its definition,
     with each $_n (where _n is a digit) replaced by the _nth argu-
     ment of the call (argument 0 is the macro name itself); a $
     not followed by a digit is passed through as itself.

